Windows Registry Editor Version 5.00

; Pins VirtualDJ's data directory ("HomeFolder") to the external SSD, so
; the song database, playlists, mappers, pads and settings live on D:
; and travel with the drive instead of sitting in the Wine prefix.
;
; D: is dosdevices/d: -> /run/media/geza/ExternalSSD, a udisks automount,
; so it is simply absent whenever the SSD is not plugged in.
;
; The problem this solves is not the missing drive itself -- VirtualDJ
; handles that, after a fashion, by falling back to its default
; C:\users\geza\AppData\Local\VirtualDJ. The problem is that it also
; *writes that fallback back into the registry*, silently discarding the
; D: setting. Nothing restores it when the drive comes back, so the next
; run with the SSD attached still reads the stale C: copy, and the real
; database on D: quietly stops being the one in use.
;
; Importing this on every launch makes that self-healing: a driveless
; session still clobbers the value, but the next launch rewrites
; D:\VirtualDJ before virtualdj.exe ever reads it. The import is
; unconditional on purpose -- when the SSD is absent VirtualDJ just
; overwrites it again, which is no worse than today, and keeping the
; launcher free of drive-state logic keeps it honest.
;
; What this does NOT do: stop a driveless session from running against
; the stale C: copy. That copy is real and populated, so edits made
; without the SSD attached land there and are invisible once D: is back.
; If that ever bites, the fix is a guard in the launcher that refuses to
; start when D:\VirtualDJ is missing, not a change here.
;
; RunFolder64 in this same key is the *install* directory
; (C:\Program Files\VirtualDJ) and is deliberately left alone -- the
; program itself is not portable, only its data.
[HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\VirtualDJ]
"HomeFolder"="D:\\VirtualDJ"
